```java
// ANTI-PATTERN: a state-changing action exposed over GET
@GetMapping("/transfer")  // GET should never change state
public String transfer(@RequestParam String to, @RequestParam BigDecimal amount) {
    accountService.transfer(currentUser(), to, amount);
    return "done";
}
```
```html
<!-- Attacker's page just needs an <img> to fire the request with cookies -->
<img src="https://bank.com/transfer?to=attacker&amount=5000">
<!-- The victim's browser sends the session cookie automatically -->
```
*Exposing state changes over GET (or POST without a token) lets any page forge the request.*

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        // CSRF protection is ON by default for cookie/session apps
        http.csrf(Customizer.withDefaults())
            .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated());
        return http.build();
    }

    @PostMapping("/transfer")  // state changes use POST
    public String transfer(@RequestParam String to, @RequestParam BigDecimal amount) {
        accountService.transfer(currentUser(), to, amount);
        return "done";
    }
}
```
```html
<!-- The server-rendered form includes a per-session token the attacker can't read -->
<form method="post" action="/transfer">
  <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" th:value="${_csrf.token}"/>
  ...
</form>
```

```java
// Cookie-based app with CSRF turned off -> forgeable
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
    .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults());
// Any cross-site POST with the session cookie now succeeds.
```

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http.csrf(csrf -> csrf
        // Expose token via a cookie JS can read (for SPA), validate on each write
        .csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse()));
    return http.build();
}
```
```html
<!-- Server-rendered form: token is injected and sent back on submit -->
<form method="post" action="/account/email">
  <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" th:value="${_csrf.token}"/>
  <input name="email"/>
</form>
```
```js
// SPA: read the XSRF-TOKEN cookie and echo it in a header the attacker can't set
fetch('/account/email', { method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'X-XSRF-TOKEN': readCookie('XSRF-TOKEN') }, body });
```

```java
// Storing the JWT in a cookie AND disabling CSRF = forgeable again
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())            // removed the protection...
    .addFilterBefore(jwtCookieFilter,        // ...but token rides in a cookie
        UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);
// Browser auto-attaches the JWT cookie to cross-site requests -> CSRF.
```

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        // Token comes from the Authorization header, never auto-sent -> no CSRF
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
        .sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
        .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}
```
```js
// Client must explicitly attach the token — a cross-site page cannot do this
fetch('/api/orders', { method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Authorization': 'Bearer ' + token } });
```
*Disabling CSRF is correct here precisely because there is no auto-sent credential to forge.*

```java
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    http
        .sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
        // Credentials come from the Authorization header, never auto-sent
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
        .addFilterBefore(jwtAuthFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .authorizeHttpRequests(a -> a.anyRequest().authenticated());
    return http.build();
}
// Stateless + header token => no ambient cookie => disabling CSRF is correct.
```
